<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="notes-week-08">
  <title>Week 8: Limits of Integrals and Integrals of Limits (3B) </title>


<subsection xml:id="subsec-Bounded-Convergence-Theroem">
  <title>Bounded Convergence Theorem</title>

  <p>
    Suppose that $\{f_n\}$ is a sequence of integrable functions that converges to a limit function $f$. An important question with many applications is when is it true that
    <md>
       \lim_{n\to \infty} \int f_n d\mu = \int f d\mu;
    </md>
    in other words, when are we permitted to exchange limit and integral?
  </p>

  <p>
     In MTH 528 we saw that when the integral is the Riemann integral, if convergence is uniform, then we are permitted to exchange limit and integral. Can we do better with the Lebesgue Integral?
  </p>

  <p>
    Example: Let $r_n$ be a (countable) list of the rational numbers and define functions $f_n(x)$ by 
    <md>
      f_n(x) = \begin{cases} 
      \hfill  1 \amp \text{ if $x \in \{r_1, r_2, \ldots, r_n\}$} \\
      \hfill  0 \hfill \amp \text{ otherwise} 
  \end{cases}
    </md>
    
  </p>

  <p>
    Each $f_n$ is $0$ except at a finite number of points, so it is Riemann integrable with integral $0$, but $f_n$ converges pointwise to Dirichlet's function 
    <md>
       f(x) = \begin{cases} 
      \hfill  1 \amp \text{ if $x \in \mathbf{Q}$} \\
      \hfill  0 \hfill \amp \text{ if $x \notin \mathbf{Q}$} 
  \end{cases}
    </md>
    which is not Riemann integrable. However, it is Lebesgue integrable with integral $0$.
  </p>

  <p>
    Example: Let
    <md>
      g_n(x) = \begin{cases} 
      \hfill  n \amp \text{ if $0 &lt; x &lt; \frac{1}{n}$} \\
      \hfill  0 \hfill \amp \text{ if $x =0 $ or $x \geq \frac{1}{n}$} 
  \end{cases}
    </md>
    
  </p>

  <p>
    For each $n$, $g_n(x)$ is Lebesgue integrable with integral $1$; however, $\{g_n\}$ converges pointwise to $g(x) = 0$, which has Lebesgue integral $0$, so we can't exchange limit and integral. Notice that in the first example $|f_n(x)| \leq 1$ for all $n$, but in the second example $\{g_n\}$ is unbounded.
  </p>

  <theorem xml:id="thm-BCT">
    <title>Bounded Convergence Theorem</title>
    
    
    <statement>
      <p>
        Suppose $(X,S,\mu)$ is a measure space with $\mu(X) &lt; \infty$. Suppose $f_1, f_2, \ldots$ is a sequence of $S$-measurable functions from $X \to \mathbf{R}$ that converges pointwise on $X$ to a function $f:X \to \mathbf{R}$. If there exists $c \in (0,\infty)$ such that 
        <md>
          |f_k(x)| \leq c
        </md>
        for all $k \in \mathbf{N}$ and $x \in X$, then
        <md>
          \lim_{k\to \infty} \int f_k d\mu = \int f d\mu.
        </md>
        
      </p>
    </statement>
  </theorem>
  

  <lemma xml:id="lem-integral-bound">
  <title>Bounding an Integral</title>
  
  
    <statement>
      <p>
        Suppose $(X,S, \mu)$ is a measure space, $E$ is a subset in $S$, and $f:X \to [-\infty,\infty]$ is a function such that $\int_E f d\mu$ is defined. Then
        <md>
          \int_E f d\mu \leq \mu(E) \sup_E |f|.
        </md>
        
      </p>
    </statement>
  </lemma>
  
  <proof>
    <p>
    Let $c = \sup_E |f|$. Then
    <md>
      \int_E f d\mu \leq \int_E c d\mu  = c \int_E d\mu = c \mu(E).
    </md>
    
  </p>
  </proof>

  <p>
    Now we can prove the Bounded Convergence Theorem
  </p>

  <proof>
    <p>
      The main idea of the proof is that by Egorov's Theorem, every convergent sequence is almost uniformly convergent and if convergence is uniform then you can interchange limit and integral.
    </p>

<p>
  Let $\epsilon &gt; 0$. Then by Egorov's Theorem there is a subset $E \in S$ such that $\mu(X \setminus E) &lt; \epsilon$ and $f_1, f_2, \ldots$ converges uniformly to $f$ on $E$. Then
  <md>
    <mrow>  \left|\int f_k d\mu - \int f d\mu\right| \amp = \left| \int_{X \setminus E} f_k d\mu - \int_{X\setminus E} f d\mu + \int_E (f_k-f) d\mu \right| </mrow>
    <mrow> \amp \leq \int_{X\setminus E} |f_k| d\mu + \int_{X\setminus E} |f| d\mu + \int_E |f_k -f| d\mu </mrow>
    <mrow> \amp &lt; 2c \epsilon + \mu(E) \sup_E |f_k - f|,</mrow>
  </md>
  where the last line follows from applying the lemma.
</p>

<p>
  Since $\{f_k\}$ converges uniformly to $f$ on $E$, it follows that for large $k$, $|f_k-f| &lt; \epsilon$. Hence for $k$ sufficiently large,
  <md>
    \left|\int f_k d\mu - \int f d\mu\right| &lt; 2c \epsilon + \mu(E) \epsilon,
  </md>
  and since $\epsilon &gt; 0$ is arbitrary this can be made as small as possible. Thus $\int f_k d\mu \to \int f d\mu$ as $k \to \infty$.
</p>


  </proof>


</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-Sets-of-Measure-Zero-in-Integration">
  <title>Sets of Measure Zero in Integration</title>
  
  <p>
    Example: Suppose that
    <md>
      f_n(x) = \begin{cases} 
      \hfill  n \amp \text{ if $x \in [0,1] \cap \mathbf{Q} $} \\
       \hfill  \frac{x}{n} \hfill \amp \text{ if $x \in [0,1] \setminus \mathbf{Q} $} 
  \end{cases}
    </md>
    
  </p>

  <p>
    Then $\{f_n\}$ converges pointwise to 
    <md>
      f(x) = \begin{cases} 
      \hfill  \infty \amp \text{ if $x \in [0,1] \cap \mathbf{Q} $} \\
       \hfill  0 \hfill \amp \text{ if $x \in [0,1] \setminus \mathbf{Q} $} 
  \end{cases}
    </md>
    but both $\{f_n\}$ and $f$ are unbounded, so we can't use Bounded Convergence Theorem, or can we?
  </p>

  <p>
    Note that $\{f_n\}$ is bounded almost everywhere; that is, it is only unbounded on a set of measure zero and since sets of measure zero are negligible with respect to integration we can still use BCT.
  </p>

  <p>
    Define a new function $g_n(x)$ by
    <md>
      g_n(x) = \begin{cases} 
      \hfill  0 \amp \text{ if $x \in [0,1] \cap \mathbf{Q} $} \\
       \hfill  \frac{x}{n} \hfill \amp \text{ if $x \in [0,1] \setminus \mathbf{Q} $} 
       \end{cases}
    </md>
    
  </p>

  <p>
    Then $\{g_n\}$ converges pointwise to <m>g(x) = 0</m>. Since $\{g_n\}$ is bounded by $1$, we can use BCT to conclude that
    <md>
      \int_0^1 g_n d\mu = \int_0^1 g d\mu = 0.
    </md>
    
  </p>

  <p>
    Since
    <md>
      \int_0^1 f_k d\mu = \int_0^1 g_k d\mu \text{ and } \int_0^1 f d\mu = \int_0^1 g d\mu
    </md>
    it follows that
    <md>
       \int_0^1 f_k d\mu = \int_0^1 f d\mu = 0.
    </md>
    
  </p>

  <p>
    The ideas of this example can be universally applied to theorems about integrals. In most every case, the hypotheses of a theorem about integrals can be relaxed so that the hypotheses just hold almost everywhere; that is, outside a set of measure zero. The previous example shows that the BCT still holds if the sequence of functions is bounded almost everywhere on $X$ instead of needing to be bounded on all $X$.
  </p>



</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-Dominated-Convergence-Theorem">
  <title>Dominated Convergence Theorem</title>

  <p>
    The BCT says that we can interchange limit and integral if the sequence of functions is uniformly bounded and the measure of the space is finite. The MCT says that we can interchange limit and integral if the functions are non-negative and increasing. The Dominated Convergence Theorem does away with these restrictions. The only requirement for the DCT is that the sequence of functions can be bounded by another integrable function.
  </p>

  <theorem xml:id="thm-DCT">
    <title>Dominated Convergence Theorem</title>
    
    
    <statement>
      <p>
        Suppose $(X,S,\mu)$ is a measure space, $f:X \to [-\infty,\infty]$ is $S$-measurable, and $f_1, f_2, \ldots$ are $S$-measurable functions from $X \to [-\infty,\infty]$ such that 
        <md>
          \lim_{k\to \infty} f_k(x) = f(x)
        </md>
        for almost every $x \in X$. If there exists an $S$-measurable function $g:X \to [0,\infty]$ such that
        <md>
          \int g d\mu &lt; \infty \text{ and } |f_K(x)| \leq g(x)
        </md>
        for every $k \in \mathbf{N}$ and almost every $x \in X$, then
        <md>
          \lim_{k \to \infty} f_k d\mu = \int f d\mu.
        </md>
        
      </p>
    </statement>
  </theorem>
  
  
</subsection>

  </section>